<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Is a 25 dB loss bad?</h3>
<p>Many charts call 0–20 dB HL “normal.” Around 25 dB HL is often labeled “borderline” or “very mild.” Even small changes can matter, though—especially for hearing soft voices, children’s speech, or in noise. If you’re struggling, don’t wait for the numbers to get bigger; talk with an audiologist about strategies, communication tweaks, and whether amplification could help.</p>

<h3>Why is my word score low if my audiogram looks mild?</h3>
<p>Pure tones and speech understanding measure different things. You might hear beeps fine but still have reduced clarity due to damaged synapses or hair cells that affect timing and detail, especially in noise. That’s why speech testing (including speech-in-noise) is essential. The fix is often a mix of well-fitted hearing aids, accessories like remote mics, and listening strategies.</p>

<h3>Can an audiogram miss hidden hearing loss?</h3>
<p>Yes. Standard audiograms measure the quietest sounds you detect in quiet, not how well your auditory system handles complex, noisy scenes. People can have normal thresholds but struggle in noise. Speech-in-noise tests and a careful case history help uncover these issues, and training plus tech can improve day-to-day listening.</p>

<h3>Do hearing aids improve my audiogram?</h3>
<p>They don’t change your measured thresholds, but they improve what reaches your brain. With the right fitting and features, you can regain access to soft consonants, boost speech-in-noise performance, and reduce listening fatigue. Think of hearing aids as precision glasses for sound—worn during life, not during the test.</p>


